\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
A Short Review Of The 1972 Freedman-Clauser And 1982 Aspect Experiments:
 An Empirical Mapping of Continuous Attenuation Functions vs.
 Discrete Binary Products
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Roger L.
 Mc Murtrie
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Email:
 rogermc@iinet.net.au
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
This article re-examines the results of the 1972 Freedman-Clauser 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 experiments and the 1982 Aspect et.
 al.
 experiments 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Aspect82"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Particular attention is given to the mathematical relationship between the experimentally measured photon coincidence rates and the theoretical expressions used in interpreting those measurements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The analysis follows the original mathematical formulations,
 examining the definitions of the experimental observables,
 the derivation of their expected values and the assumptions underlying the application of Bell inequalities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
It is argued that the excellent experimental agreement with the predicted angular dependence of coincidence rates must be distinguished from the separate question of whether or not those measurements establish a violation of local realism.
 The implications of this distinction for the interpretation of the reported experimental results are discussed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Stuart Freedman's thesis 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and Alain Aspect's article 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Aspect82"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 both describe the measurement of the polarization correlation of two photons emitted in the 
\begin_inset Formula $4p^{2}\;^{1}S_{0}\rightarrow4s4p^{1}P_{1}\rightarrow4s^{2}\;^{1}S_{0}$
\end_inset

 cascade of calcium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results of both experiments are in excellent agreement with their relevant expectation values.
 Unfortunately this situation is somewhat marred by comparisons with other inapplicable expectation values.
 In accordance with the scientific method,
 a statistically significant disagreement between experimental observations and theoretical predictions calls for investigation.
 Such disagreement may arise from one or more of the following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Experimental limitations:
 The experimental arrangement,
 measurement procedures or statistical treatment of the observations may be inadequate or inappropriate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Inapplicability of the theory:
 The assumptions or conditions under which the theoretical prediction was derived may not be satisfied by the experimental arrangement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Errors in the theoretical derivation:
 The mathematical reasoning leading to the theoretical prediction may contain errors or unjustified assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These possibilities are not mutually exclusive and should be examined before conclusions are drawn concerning the validity or applicability of the theoretical model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the case of the experiments investigated in this article it aappears to be the second of these possibilities that is responsible for disagreement.
 The experimental results investigated are numeric coincidence rates whereas the inequalities that they are compared with are derived from and are only applicable to experiments in which the experimental results are binary values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Two-Photon System Investigated
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The systems investigated by Freedman's and Aspect's experiments both comprise beams of distinguishable pairs of photons emitted in an atomic cascade.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To a good approximation the atom cascades via electric dipole transitions through angular-momentum states 
\begin_inset Formula $J=0\rightarrow J=1\rightarrow J=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The initial and final atomic angular momentum.
 is zero;
 hence,
 by conservation of angular momentum,
 the two photons are in a combined state of zero angular momentum meaning that their polarization vectors are correlated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Relevant Theoretical Framework
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman states that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the quantum-mechanical expectation value for the product of individual spin measurements in the directions 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{b}$
\end_inset

 is given by the following expectation value
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (I.2)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right) & = & \left\langle \psi\left|\hat{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{1}}\,\hat{b}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{2}}\right|\psi\right\rangle =-\hat{a}\cdot\hat{b}\label{eq:FI.2}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In general,
 the symbology 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(...\right)$
\end_inset

 denotes an expectation value.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 represents the wave functions of two particles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\overrightarrow{\sigma_{1}}\,$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\overrightarrow{\sigma_{2}}$
\end_inset

 represents the vector of three Pauli matrices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:FI.2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 is actually the expectation value 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(\hat{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{1}}\,\hat{b}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{2}}\right)$
\end_inset

 of the observables 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{1}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{b}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{2}}$
\end_inset

 operating on the wave function 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 and is more correctly written:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\left(\hat{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{1}}\right)\,\left(\hat{b}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{2}}\right)\right) & = & \left\langle \psi\left|\left(\hat{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{1}}\right)\,\left(\hat{b}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{2}}\right)\right|\psi\right\rangle =-\hat{a}\cdot\hat{b}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 the experiments described herein measure neither 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{1}}$
\end_inset

 nor 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{b}\cdot\overrightarrow{\sigma_{2}}$
\end_inset

 but,
 effectively,
 do measure the positive and negative polarity of these observables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (I.9)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the expectation value for the joint polarization measurements of the observables 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 over the domain,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varGamma$
\end_inset

,
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{2}$
\end_inset

 should be written:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(O_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)O_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)\right)_{\varGamma} & = & \left\langle \gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\left|O_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)O_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)\right|\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\right\rangle =cos\left(2\phi\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The expectation value of the product 
\begin_inset Formula $A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 over the domain 
\begin_inset Formula $\varLambda$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)\right)_{\Lambda} & = & \int_{\Lambda}\rho\left(\lambda\right)A\left(\hat{a},\lambda_{1}\right)B\left(\hat{b},\lambda_{2}\right)d\lambda_{2}d\lambda_{1}\label{eq:FII.2-1}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 represents the random variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\left(\lambda\right)$
\end_inset

 represents the probability distribution function of the random variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As shown in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "subsec:Derivation-of-Bell-inequality"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

Freedman derives the following inequalities from equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:FII.2-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation(II.6)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
-2\leq & P_{++}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)+P_{++}\left(\hat{d},\hat{c}\right)+P_{++}\left(\hat{d},\hat{b}\right)-P_{1}\left(\hat{d}\right)-P_{2}\left(\hat{b}\right) & \leq2\label{eq:FII.6-1}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman states the probability (actually expectation value) that both photons are detected as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{R\left(\phi\right)}{R_{0}} & = & \left\langle \gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\left|\pi_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)\pi_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)\right|\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\right\rangle \label{eq:FII.9}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 represents the angle between the two polarizer orientations,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the polarizer orientation of polarizer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{2}$
\end_inset

 represent the individual wave functions of a particle-pair,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{i}\left(\phi_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 represents the the transmission characteristics of polarizer (i) for of a photon through a polarizer with polarization axis in the x direction 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{i}$
\end_inset

 which is represented by the matrix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:pi_i"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The right side of equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:FII.9"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 in standard quantum theoretical symbology,
 actually represents an expectation value (predicted value),
 not a probability 
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Off-diagonal matrix elements describe transition probabilities between different states.
 Diagonal matrix elements represent expectation values which are the predicted statistical averages of measurements of an observable across an ensemble of identical quantum systems.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\pi_{i} & = & \begin{bmatrix}\varepsilon^{i}_{M} & 0\\
0 & \varepsilon^{i}_{m}
\end{bmatrix}\label{eq:pi_i}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
0\leq\epsilon_{mi}\leq\epsilon_{Mi}\leq1.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon^{i}_{M}$
\end_inset

 represents the transmission efficiency of polarizer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon^{i}_{m}$
\end_inset

 represents the non-transmission efficiency of polarizer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The derivation of 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(\pi_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)\pi_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)\right)_{\Lambda}$
\end_inset

 is given in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Derivation-Of-Equation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Defining 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{R\left(\phi\right)}{R_{0}}$
\end_inset

 as the experimentally measured ratio given by Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:FII.9"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 the predicted ratio of the measured count rate 
\begin_inset Formula $R\left(\phi\right)$
\end_inset

 with polarizers to the measured count rate 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{0}$
\end_inset

 without polarizers the expected value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{R\left(\phi\right)}{R_{0}}$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\frac{R\left(\phi\right)}{R_{0}}\right) & = & E\left(\pi_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)\pi_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)\right)_{\Lambda}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This assumption provides the statistical null-hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\frac{R\left(\phi\right)}{R_{0}}\right) & = & \left\langle \gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\left|\pi_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)\pi_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)\right|\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\right\rangle 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman also defines:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\frac{R_{1}}{R_{0}}\right) & = & \frac{1}{2}\left(\varepsilon^{1}_{M}+\varepsilon^{1}_{m}\right)\label{eq:FII.11a}\\
E\left(\frac{R_{2}}{R_{0}}\right) & = & \frac{1}{2}\left(\varepsilon^{2}_{M}+\varepsilon^{2}_{m}\right)\label{eq:FI.11b}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman's derivation of the following inequalities 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "inequality (II.8)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is given in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "sec:Derivation-And-Applicabiliry"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
-1\leq & \frac{R\left(\phi_{1}\right)}{R_{0}}-\frac{R\left(\phi_{1}+\phi_{2}+\phi_{3}\right)}{R_{0}}+\frac{R\left(\phi_{2}\right)}{R_{0}}+\frac{R\left(\phi_{3}\right)}{R_{0}}-\frac{R_{1}}{R_{0}}-\frac{R_{2}}{R_{0}} & \leq0\label{eq:FII.8}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\phi_{1} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{a},\hat{b}\right)\\
\phi_{2} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{d},\hat{c}\right)\\
\phi_{3} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{d},\hat{b}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\phi_{1}+\phi_{2}+\phi_{3} & = & cos^{-1}\left(\hat{a},\hat{c}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R\left(\phi_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 represents the coincidence rate with both polarizers in place.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R_{0}$
\end_inset

 represents the coincidence rate with both polarizers removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R_{1}$
\end_inset

 represents the coincidence rate with polarizer 2 removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $R_{2}$
\end_inset

 represents the coincidence rate with polarizer 1 removed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Freedman's inequality 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:FII.8"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 is initially derived in terms of theoretical joint and marginal probabilities.
 These probabilities are subsequently expressed in terms of experimentally measured coincidence-rate ratios,
 such as 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{R(\phi)}{R_{0}}$
\end_inset

.
 The derivation of the probability inequality and its subsequent application to the experimental measurements involves two distinct steps:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
1.
 
\series bold
Derivation of the theoretical inequality
\series default
.
 Freedman derives inequality 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:FII.8"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 in terms of theoretical joint and marginal probabilities including 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{++}\left(A\left(\overrightarrow{a}\right)B\left(\overrightarrow{b}\right)\right)$
\end_inset

 etc..
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
2.
 
\series bold
Application to experimental measurements.

\series default
 This involves expressing the theoretical probabilities in terms of experimentally measured coincidence-rate ratios including 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{R(\phi}{R_{0}}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{R_{1}}{R_{0}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{R_{2}}{R_{0}}$
\end_inset

 enabling the inequality to be evaluated using experimental results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Expectation values can be expressed as a four-quadrant matrix:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E_{\Lambda} & = & \begin{bmatrix}E_{\varLambda}\left(A\left(\overrightarrow{a}\right)B\left(\overrightarrow{b}\right)\right) & E_{\varLambda}\left(A\left(\overrightarrow{a}\right)B\left(\overrightarrow{d}\right)\right)\\
E_{\varLambda}\left(A\left(\overrightarrow{c}\right)B\left(\overrightarrow{b}\right)\right) & E_{\varLambda}\left(A\left(\overrightarrow{c}\right)B\left(\overrightarrow{d}\right)\right)
\end{bmatrix}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Aspect et.
 al.
 define the theoretical expectation value 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (1)"
key "Aspect82"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{b}\right) & = & P_{++}\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{b}\right)+P_{--}\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{b}\right)-P_{+-}\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{b}\right)-P_{-+}\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{b}\right)\label{eq:A1}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{b}\right)$
\end_inset

 means 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(A\left(\overrightarrow{a}\right)B\left(\overrightarrow{b}\right)\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Allowing for experimental restrictions 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (5)"
key "Aspect82"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{b}\right) & = & F\frac{\left(T^{\Vert}_{1}-T^{\bot}_{1}\right)\left(T^{\Vert}_{2}-T^{\bot}_{2}\right)}{\left(T^{\Vert}_{1}+T^{\bot}_{1}\right)\left(T^{\Vert}_{2}+T^{\bot}_{2}\right)}cos\left(2\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{b}\right)\right)\label{eq:A5}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 represents a factor that accounts for the finite solid detection angles,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $T^{\Vert}_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the transmission coefficient for the parallel channel of polarizer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $T^{\bot}_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the transmission coefficient for the perpendicular channel of polarizer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Aspect et.
 al.
 define a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generalized Bell's inequality
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (2)"
key "Aspect82"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
-2\leq & S & \leq2\label{eq:A2}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
S & = & E\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{b}\right)-E\left(\overrightarrow{a},\overrightarrow{d}\right)+E\left(\overrightarrow{c},\overrightarrow{b}\right)+E\left(\overrightarrow{c},\overrightarrow{d}\right)\label{eq:A2-1}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Based on equations 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:A1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:A2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
