\begin_layout Section
Discussion And Conclusions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two separate conclusions to examine:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- Whether or not the measured coincidence rates agree with the theoretical predictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
- Whether or not those measurements,
 together with the assumptions used in their analysis,
 establish a violation of the relevant local-hidden-variable inequality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These are logically distinct questions.
 Keeping them separate would strengthen the argument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Neither Freedman's nor Aspect's experiment satisfies the fundamental probability constraints mandated by Bell’s 1964 Equation (2).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both experiments the empirical data points track a smooth,
 solid continuous curve 
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{\langle R(\phi)/R_{0}\rangle}$
\end_inset

 because the laboratory instruments are measuring continuous macroscopic wave intensities and timing intervals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The differing graphical forms of Freedman's Figure 21 and Aspect et al.'s Figure 3 arise principally from the different experimental observables employed.
 Freedman's measurements were expressed as normalized coincidence rates associated with transmission through single-channel polarizers.
 Aspect et al.,
 employing two-channel polarimeters,
 constructed polarization correlation coefficients from the four possible combinations of detected outcomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although both quantities exhibit an angular dependence involving 
\begin_inset Formula $(\cos(2\phi),$
\end_inset

 their mathematical definitions and normalization procedures differ.
 In particular,
 the interpretation of Aspect's experimentally constructed correlation coefficients as the theoretical correlations entering the Bell inequality depends upon the assumption that the detected photon pairs constitute a representative sample of the emitted pairs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Continuous profile divergence from standard CHSH inequalities mathematically demonstrates that the inequality is completely inapplicable to this class of data;
 not that nature has violated local realistic probability logic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to the experimental results investigated herein the article 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Aspect1991"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 also involves an inapplicable comparison.
 In this case,
 as for the other two,
 experimental results appear in excellent agreement with predicted results but the article contains an inapplicable comparison with an inequality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unfortunately the statistical concept and definition of expectation value appears to be widely misunderstood or ignored by many within the physics community.
\end_layout

