\begin_layout Section
Measurement Of Photon Detection Rates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Clicks Enter:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Photons hit Detector A and Detector B each of which converts the light into an electrical pulse—
a raw "click".
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Gating Circuit:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

These pulses travel down coaxial cables into a coincidence circuit which acts like a logical AND gate with a built-in stopwatch.
 If pulse A and pulse B arrive within about 5 to 30 nanoseconds of each other the circuit emits a single "coincidence pulse".
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Counter (The Scaler):
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The coincidence pulse travels into an electronic counter (historically called a scaler).
 It does not calculate rates dynamically;
 it just counts.
 It ticks up:
 1,
 2,
 3,
 4...
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Reporting the Rate:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

To get the rate a mechanical timer collects clicks for a fixed period of time (say,
 100 seconds).
 If the counter reads 2,400 when the timer went off the reported rate for that run is written down as 24 clicks per second.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Effectively,
 the device counts individual,
 discrete events over a fixed timeframe and that total count is converted into a "rate" because it is an easy way to write down the data and normalize it against baseline data 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(R_{0}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Derivation Of The Expected Value 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left(\pi_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)\pi_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)\right)_{\Lambda}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Derivation-Of-Equation"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This derivation follows Freedman's mathematical treatment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a representation that exploits the binary character of photon polarization 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (I.7)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left|\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\right\rangle  & = & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left[\begin{pmatrix}1\\
0
\end{pmatrix}_{1}\begin{pmatrix}1\\
0
\end{pmatrix}_{2}-\begin{pmatrix}0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}_{1}\begin{pmatrix}0\\
1
\end{pmatrix}_{2}\right]\label{eq:FI.7}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this representation a measurement of a photon's linear polarization in an arbitrary direction perpendicular to the z axis is associated with an hermitian operator of the form 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (I.8)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
O_{i}\left(\phi_{i}\right) & = & \begin{bmatrix}cos\left(2\phi_{i}\right) & sin\left(2\phi_{i}\right)\\
sin\left(2\phi_{i}\right) & -cos\left(2\phi_{i}\right)
\end{bmatrix}\label{eq:FI.8}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The expectation value for joint polarization measurements is 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "equation (I.9)"
key "Freedman1972"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\phi\right) & = & \left\langle \gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\left|O_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)O_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)\right|\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\right\rangle =cos\left(2\phi\right)\label{eq:FI.9}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 represents the angle between the polarizer orientations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The observation of transmission or non-transmission of a photon through a polarizer with polarization axis in the x direction 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{i}$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\pi_{i} & = & \begin{bmatrix}\varepsilon^{i}_{M} & 0\\
0 & \varepsilon^{i}_{m}
\end{bmatrix}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\varepsilon^{i}_{M}>\varepsilon^{i}_{m}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Transforming 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{i}$
\end_inset

 by a rotation 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}$
\end_inset

 about the z axis defines an operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{i}\left(\phi_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 for arbitrary for an polarizer orientation as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\pi_{i}\left(\phi_{i}\right) & = & \begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{2}\left(\varepsilon^{i}_{M}+\varepsilon^{i}_{m}\right)+\frac{1}{2}\left(\varepsilon^{i}_{M}-\varepsilon^{i}_{m}\right)cos\left(2\phi_{i}\right) & \frac{1}{2}\left(\varepsilon^{i}_{M}-\varepsilon^{i}_{m}\right)sin\left(2\phi_{i}\right)\\
\frac{1}{2}\left(\varepsilon^{i}_{M}-\varepsilon^{i}_{m}\right)sin\left(2\phi_{i}\right) & \frac{1}{2}\left(\varepsilon^{i}_{M}+\varepsilon^{i}_{m}\right)+\frac{1}{2}\left(\varepsilon^{i}_{M}-\varepsilon^{i}_{m}\right)cos\left(2\phi_{i}\right)
\end{bmatrix}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{2}$
\end_inset

 are defined in different right-handed coordinate frames.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The expectation value that both photons are transmitted is defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(\pi_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)\pi_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)\right)_{\Lambda} & = & \left\langle \gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\left|\pi_{1}\left(\phi_{1}\right)\pi_{2}\left(\phi_{2}\right)\gamma_{1}\gamma_{2}\right|\right\rangle \label{eq:FII.9-1}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the wave function of photon 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda$
\end_inset

 represents the population domain of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

